$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):EXP_PROC
$DATUM Benzylic Bromides 2a-f; Genearl Procedure NBS (10.8 g, 60 mmol) was added
in three equal portions during 9 h to a solution of 1 (10 mmol) in
refluxing CCl4 (60 mL), each addition being followed by a few milligrams
of benzoyl peroxide. The mixture was cooled and filtered to remove the
succinimide. The filtrate was washed with aq NaHCO3 solution (30 mL) and
brine (30 mL). After drying (Na2SO4), the solvent was evaporated under
reduced pressure and the residue was dissolved in anhyd THF (20 mL). To
the resulting solution were added diethyl phosphite (15.4 mL, 120 mmol)
and i-Pr2NEt (20.8 mL, 120 mmol) at 0 °C under Ar with stirring. The
mixture was gradually warmed to r.t. and stirred for 29 h (the reaction
was monitored by 1H NMR). The mixture was poured into ice/water and
extracted with Et2O (3 x 40 mL). The organic layer was washed with 1 M
HCl (except for 2e) and brine, dried (Na2SO4), filtered and evaporated to
give the crude product. The pure product was obtained by silica gel
column chromatography with petroleum ether as eluent (Tables 1 and 2).
2a, yield 75%. Bp 60-90 °C. Mp: 84-85°C.1H NMR (CDCl3) δ, J Hz: 2.26 (s,
3 H), 4.40 (s, 4 H), 7.04 (d, 2 H, J = 1.6), 7.23 (t, 1 H, J = 1.6). MS
(70 eV, EI) M/z (%): 322 (M + 2, 5), 280 (37), 241 (16), 199 (56), 120
(32), 91 (29), 43 (100). IR (KBr) cm-1: 1764 (C=O), 1614, 1589, 1451,
1369, 1296, 1203, 1141, 1022, 982, 913, 693.
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):TITLE
$DATUM An efficient method for the preparation of benzylic bromides
$DTYPE RXN:VAR(1):REFERENCE(1):AUTHOR
